2007:
(a) The purpose of this article is to:
(1) Establish a uniform permitting and inspection process, and reasonable regulations for encroachment permits for the installation, construction, maintenance, renewal, removal, or relocation of pipes, mains, conduits, cables, wires, poles, towers, traffic and other signals, and other equipment, facilities, or appliances of any utility in, on, along, over, or under the public roads, rights-of-way, and permanent easements that are a part of the county road system lying outside the corporate limits of a municipality, hereinafter collectively referred to as "the county's public roads;"
(2) Establish a uniform permitting and inspection process, and reasonable regulations for encroachment permits for the construction, maintenance, relocation, or removal of driveways, sidewalks, curbing, bike lanes, multi-use trails, associated drainage facilities, and landscaping in, on, along, over, or under the county's public roads;
(3) Efficiently manage use of the county's public roads and maintain and preserve the physical integrity and layout of the county's public roads;
(4) Ensure that work is planned and performed in a manner that does not cause damage and does not create hazardous conditions in, on, along, over, or under the county's public roads;
(5) Inspect and monitor work to ensure that appropriate signage, barricades, and lighting are utilized to protect the public when work is in progress on the county's public roads;
(6) Inspect and monitor work so that it is performed in a safe manner that reduces the risk of injury to persons using the county's public roads;
(7) Inspect and monitor work to reduce damage to private property, including but not limited to cars, motorcycles, and bicycles traveling on the county's public roads;
(8) Inspect and monitor work to reduce damage to water and sewer lines, sidewalks, rights-of-way, or road surfaces;
(9) Regulate the time and location of excavation and other work to preserve effective traffic flow and prevent hazardous road conditions; and
(10) Recover a share of the cost of processing permits, inspecting and monitoring permitted work, and tracking repairs and paving made necessary due to repeated excavation associated with permitted activity.
(b) If there is a conflict between the provisions and requirements in this article, and federal law, state law, and/or any applicable state regulations adopted by the Georgia Department of Transportation ("GDOT"), as they exist now or may be amended hereafter, then federal law, state law, and/or the GDOT regulations shall govern.
(Ord. No. 12-16, Pt. I, 10-23-12)
